('prayer-request-management-church',
 'How to Manage Prayer Requests in Your Church Effectively',
 'Prayer is the engine of the church — but prayer requests that get lost, forgotten, or never followed up on discourage the people who shared them. This guide shows how to build a prayer request system that actually works for your congregation.',
 E'Prayer is one of the most intimate things a church member can share. When someone submits a prayer request — for a sick child, a lost job, a struggling marriage — they are trusting their church community with something deeply personal.\n\nWhen that request gets lost, forgotten, or never followed up on, it damages trust in a way that is very hard to repair.\n\n## The Problem with Paper Prayer Requests\n\nMost Liberian churches collect prayer requests on slips of paper during service. The pastor reads them out, prays over them, and then — what happens to the slips? In most churches, they are thrown away, filed in a drawer never to be reopened, or simply lost.\n\nNobody follows up. Nobody knows if the situation was resolved. Nobody checks in the following week.\n\n## Building a Prayer Ministry That Follows Through\n\n### Collect digitally\n\nAllow members to submit prayer requests through the church app, by SMS, or via a QR code on the bulletin. This creates a permanent, searchable record.\n\n### Assign to a prayer team\n\nNot every prayer request needs to go to the pastor. Build a prayer team — deacons, deaconesses, cell group leaders — who are responsible for following up on specific requests.\n\n### Follow up within the week\n\nA simple follow-up message — "We prayed for your mother this week. How is she doing?" — makes an enormous difference to the person who submitted the request.\n\n### Track updates\n\nWhen a prayer is answered, celebrate it. Answered prayers build faith — both for the person who was prayed for and for the whole congregation when testimonies are shared.\n\n### Respect confidentiality\n\nSome prayer requests are deeply private. Members should be able to mark a request as "personal/confidential" so it goes only to the pastor and not to the wider prayer team.\n\n## How ChurchFlow Handles Prayer Requests\n\nChurchFlow Liberia has a built-in prayer request module. Members submit requests through the member portal. Requests are visible to pastoral staff, who can assign them, add notes, and mark them as answered.\n\nAll requests are stored securely and are visible only to authorised staff — never to other members.\n\n---\n\n*Build a prayer ministry that actually follows through. Start with ChurchFlow Liberia.*',
 'Samuel Kwame', 'CEO', 'Ministry Growth',
 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1504052434569-70ad5836ab65?w=1200&q=80',
 '6 min read', true, '2026-05-06 08:00:00+00'),

